1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,700
After a crash, the first real job is documentation. Which cars,
which damage, which road, and what actually happened. ClaimScene
turns accident photos into a case a claims handler can trust.
Honest, illustrated, and never faked.

2
00:00:14,700 --> 00:00:31,133
That matters now, because generative video made accident scenes
easy to fake and hard to trust. A convincing AI reconstruction
that is really just a guess is dangerous as evidence. Insurers
and courts are already warning about synthetic media in claims.

3
00:00:31,133 --> 00:00:36,300
So ClaimScene splits every case into two layers, and it never
lets them blur.

4
00:00:36,300 --> 00:00:55,033
The photos become a constrained scene the model cannot invent
coordinates in. It may only fill a closed vocabulary of enums,
clock positions, and speed bands. Deterministic code turns that
into a top-down schematic, the factual layer, where the same
input always produces the same bytes.

5
00:00:55,033 --> 00:01:08,867
Beside it sits a clearly disclosed AI illustration, watermarked,
and never merged with the facts. Both layers, and every input
photo, are sealed on Backblaze B2 with each photo's source and
license recorded.

6
00:01:08,867 --> 00:01:27,367
The middle step is the whole point. The AI only proposes the
scene. You review and adjust it with constrained dropdowns and a
twelve-position clock, never free-text coordinates, and the
schematic redraws live as you edit. AI proposes, you confirm.
Nothing is sealed until you render.

7
00:01:27,367 --> 00:01:44,900
Here is the difference. The sealed manifest proves what the AI
proposed against what the human corrected, such as changing
vehicle A's colour. Press Verify, and the SHA-256 seal
recomputes right in your browser, backed by a receipt of
fourteen named checks over the stored bytes.

8
00:01:44,900 --> 00:01:57,233
And the accuracy is measured, not asserted. The primary model
scores one hundred percent weighted field accuracy on a
committed seven-scenario evaluation set, with a dated scoreboard
living in the repo.

9
00:01:57,233 --> 00:02:15,833
Under the hood: a Gemma and Gemini vision ladder, Genblaze for
the seedream still and pixverse clip, Backblaze B2 storage, and
Cloud Run. Live where credentials exist, deterministic fakes
everywhere else, so the whole pipeline runs offline in
continuous integration.

10
00:02:15,833 --> 00:02:25,200
ClaimScene is live on Cloud Run, and the whole project is open
source. Honest accident documentation. Illustrated, and never
faked.
